The file's whole subject is what `FFI::Platform` says about the host, so
the committed `.expected` is a description of the macOS oracle:
`OS`/`ARCH`/`NAME` are `"darwin"`, `"aarch64"`, `"aarch64-darwin"`;
`LIBSUFFIX`/`LIBC` are `"dylib"` and `"libc.dylib"`; `IS_MAC` is true and
`IS_GNU` is not even defined.

On aarch64-linux-gnu zeo answers `"linux"`, `"aarch64-linux"`, `"so"`,
`"libc.so.6"`, `IS_LINUX` true and `IS_GNU` defined -- every one of them
the right answer for that host, and every one a mismatch against a
macOS-blessed expectation. The mapped names go the same way
(`FFI.map_library_name("jpeg")` is `libjpeg.dylib` there and
`libjpeg.so` here).
